          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My HbA1c is 58.",
          "answer": "I can't tell you what a result of 58 means from the sources I searched. They don't give an interpretation for that number.\n\nHere is what they do say:\n\n- **Units matter.** HbA1c is reported either as a percentage or in mmol/mol, and the two scales give very different numbers. Check which unit your lab report uses. The sources I searched don't explain how to convert between them.\n- **Targets are individual.** The glycemic targets guidance (*Type 2 diabetes: glycemic targets and monitoring intervals*) says some bodies name a single default number, around 7 percent, while others describe a range set for each person. It also says that avoiding severe low blood sugar can be a reason to relax the goal.\n- **HbA1c can be unreliable.** The screening guidance (*Type 2 diabetes: who is screened, and the diagnostic thresholds*) says the test is less accurate with some blood conditions, recent blood transfusion, anemia, pregnancy, and advanced kidney disease. In those cases, glucose-based tests are used instead.\n- **Glucose control is one part of overall risk.** The same targets guidance notes that blood pressure, cholesterol, and smoking matter alongside it.\n\nThe sources don't say whether 58 is high, low, or in a target range for you, and I can't say that from general knowledge. Please take your result to the clinician who ordered the test. Useful questions include: what unit is this in, what is my personal target, and does anything about my health change how reliable this test is?\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
