          "question": "What code would that be recorded under?",
          "answer": "The sources I searched don't say which code a result of 58 would be recorded under. Nothing in them maps an HbA1c value to a specific code.\n\nThey do describe general rules:\n\n- **Diabetes codes (E11 family):** The ICD-10-CM coding summary says E11 is a family of codes for type 2 diabetes, with extra characters for complications. It does not say when a lab value leads to this family. Only a clinician's diagnosis does that.\n- **Abnormal findings (Chapter 18, R00-R99):** This chapter covers \"abnormal clinical and laboratory findings not elsewhere classified.\" The summary says an R code is used when a finding is recorded and no more specific diagnosis has been made. It does not list a code for HbA1c.\n- **Code versions:** The same summary says codes change between years and between countries. A code that was valid before may not be valid now, so any code should be checked against a current official file.\n\nYour clinician or the lab's billing or coding staff can tell you which code was used for your result.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
